[{:task "seon.docs/a-section-reads-at-each-level" :merge "67c065320" :plan-at "parent" :plan "What exists (docs/references.md#the-wiki-in-the-store, notes/dogfood-and-docs-2026-10-25.md section 2): every page and section is mirrored on main (`seon.docs/load-tx`, reloaded by the watcher, `my.root/watch!`), and ```seon.docs/view``` already reads a section at three depths through ```seon.ref/shown```: :summary (the heading and the lead's first sentence, `seon.ref/lead-sentence`), :doc (the lead and the sections below), :code (its own text). What is missing: (1) a section's resolved references and its backlinks are facts (`:seon.section/references` and the reverse edge, `seon.docs-test/backlinks-are-a-sections-instances`) that no view shows, so an agent or the UI cannot walk from a section to its related concepts; (2) ```seon.docs/page``` has no depth, and ```my.agent/help``` shapes its own summary list beside view (a second producer of one view); (3) `(my.agent/help \"references\")` answers nil: help reads only docs/agent.md's anchors and says nothing on a miss (dogfood: three evals lost). The change: the `:seon.ref/view` schema gains :references (what the section cites, each at summary depth: an address and its first sentence, or a var and its docstring's first sentence) and :cited-by (the sections whose references hold it, at summary depth), both from the stored edges, no new attribute; `docs/page` takes the depth and answers each section through view; `my.agent/help` is `docs/page` at :summary with no argument and `docs/view` at :code with one, and its argument may be any address (``docs/vector.md#the-model``) or an anchor of the help page; a miss answers the help page's anchors and the form that lists every page's sections, never nil. Replaces help's own shaping. Cost proof: a view costs the pulls of its section and its edges, flat in the wiki's size. Check the help page in a browser through the debug page after." :fns ["my.agent/help" "seon.docs/children" "seon.do