{:task "seon.reconcile/the-judge-asks-the-test-service" :merge "330f01988" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Left over from task seon.proofs/one-prove-path. The judge's `:tested` step (`seon.reconcile/advance`) asks the agent's `:tests` op directly ({:op :tests :id agent}), beside the test service every turn uses (`seon.agent/prove!` -> `seon.prove`). So a judge's run is not recorded on main (no `seon.prove/result-tx` facts, no `:seon.proof.run`), is not joined with a session's or the turn's request for the same key, and the outside agents' `:judge` input repeats it. Correct: the judge asks `seon.prove` for the branch at the run's end with mode :judge, keyed by `seon.prove/read-digest` of the branch's program as the turn's is; its answer is the run's results; one prove path (the audit's P4: drop the reconciler's direct `:tests`, `seon.root/run-tests!`'s use by the judge). The reconciler does not hold the branch's program digest today: the agent computes it, so either the judge evaluates `(seon.agent/prove! {:mode :judge})` in the agent (one eval, as `:worked` evaluates work!) or the digest becomes a run fact the agent writes at its end. Touches the reconciler's judge flight: after the agent-loop parts merge (they change which ends are judged). Proof: a judge after a turn at the same digest is answered by the service from the turn's run (one `:seon.proof.run` fact for both), and its results are on main." :fns ["seon.agent/handle!" "seon.agent/prove!" "seon.reconcile/advance" "seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc" "seon.root/ext-op"] :sections ["docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches" "docs/merging.md#the-test-service"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.proofs/a-red-at-its-digest-is-reused" :merge "52b374f02" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Left over from task seon.proofs/one-prove-path. Only greens are stored and reused by digest (`seon.proofs/store-greens!`, `seon.proofs/greens`, `seon.proofs/stored-greens`), so a red reruns at every ask: the judge after a red turn reruns every red the turn found, and so does each next turn on an unchanged tree. Since one-prove-path a test's verdict is a function of the test and its digest alone (one per-test tick bound everywhere), so a red at a digest is as reusable as a green (AGENTS section 5: a test's result is keyed by the digest of everything it reaches; a flaky red is a defect to fix at its cause, never a reason to rerun). Correct: the test service's red facts on main (`seon.prove/result-tx`: `:seon.proof/status` and `:seon.proof/failures` at `:seon.proof/digest`) are read by `seon.agent/test-plan` like greens, and a red reused is reported as red with its recorded failures, marked `:reused`. What reads 'a red runs again' today must change with it: proofs_test's shards-split-by-namespace-and-each-result-is-stored asserts it. Perf: a red turn's judge forks nothing for its reds. Question for root: reuse reds only from the test service's facts (recorded with failures), not from the results directory (which stores greens only)." :fns ["my.agent/prove" "seon.agent/run-tests-forked" "seon.agent/test-plan" "seon.proofs/plan" "seon.proofs/stored-results" "seon.prove/result-tx"] :sections ["docs/merging.md#the-test-service"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.proofs/one-prove-path" :merge "f83b736e1" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Owner, 2026-10-25 (docs/decisions.md, root's special cases go: one prove path). What existed: a proof run's tick budget was per run, two keys by mode (`:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` for the judge, a turn's own 10x smaller one), so a test's verdict depended on where it ran and on what ran before it in the run: a proof that needed more than the turn's budget was red (`:seon.test/ticks-spent` on the running test) in every turn and green at the judge, and a cheap test after a costly one was red in one run and green alone. bin/test had no bound at all, and its greens are reused by digest by agents. And the judge forked a child even when every test it selected was green at its digest (what the turn had just run). Changed: `seon.proofs/run-tests!` (the one runner) takes a per-test bound: each test, its :each fixtures included, runs under a Jolt `run-interruptible` budget of its own inside its :each fixtures (so their teardown runs), the namespace's fixtures within the tests' budgets and one more (`seon.proofs/test-vars`, Jolt's clojure.test fixture atoms); a test past it is an `:error` report of `:seon.test/ticks-spent` naming it and the run goes on; each result records its `:ticks`. One key, `:seon.config.agent/test-ticks` (200e9, the old judge bound), per test, used by a turn, the judge and bin/test (test/test_runner.clj); 0 means none, read once in the runner. `seon.agent/run-tests-forked` forks nothing when nothing is left to run. Deleted: the turn's own key (config, two schema entries), the mode switch in `seon.agent/handle!`, `seon.agent/run-tests-here`'s run-level budget and its error assembly. Kept by ruling (a), 2026-10-22: what a turn selects (proofs and what the branch changed) and what the judge selects (the plan); the judge runs the plan less every green at its digest, so after a turn it runs only what the turn did not. Not changed: reds are not reused (a red reruns, to the same verdict now that the bound is the test's own); the judge still asks the agent's `:tests` op rather than `seon.prove` (its runs are not recorded on main as the turn's are). Correctness: one verdict per test and digest everywhere. Perf: a judge after a covering turn forks no child and runs no test (measured in seon.agent-test/one-bound-one-verdict-and-the-judge-reruns-no-green: :ran 0, no :pid); a turn may now do up to the per-test bound per selected test (its selection is the proofs and what changed)." :fns ["seon.agent/handle!" "seon.agent/run-tests-forked" "seon.agent/run-tests-here" "seon.agent/test-report" "seon.proofs/run-tests!" "seon.proofs/test-vars" "seon.root/run-tests!"] :sections ["docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant" "docs/failure-points.md#entrant-boundaries-a-defect-becomes-a-recorded-error-map" "docs/failure-points.md#failure-points-met-without-a-catch-the-edge-answers-data" "docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches" "docs/processes.md#the-agent-process" "docs/turn.md#observe" "docs/turn.md#who-runs-which-tests"] :schemas [":seon.config.agent/test-ticks"] :tasks ["seon.proofs/a-prove-runs-key-does-not-move-between-two-asks"]}
{:task "seon.cost/a-cost-proof-is-the-same-under-load" :merge "205b0dc41" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Reproduction: Found 2026-10-23 by the cutover's decider: red in a built scratch worktree of main 4bc598dc1 as well as in the fresh clone, so it is main's, not the cutover's. In a loaded 4-shard `bin/test --all` (load average about 12) `seon.vector-cost-test/a-search-reads-each-row-once` (bytes grew as n^1.96, linear allows n^1.88; per-n ratios 1.04, 1.02, 1.00) and `seon.program-cost-test/a-probed-entity-is-sought-not-scanned` (ticks n^0.62, log allows n^0.60; ratios 0.89-0.94) went red; run alone, each is green in the same tree and in main. AGENTS section 5 says ticks and bytes are exact and the same on any machine, so a number that moves with load is measuring something else (bytes allocated by a concurrent thread? a GC in the window?). Find what moves and exclude it from the count, or widen the fit's tolerance on the exponent with the measured spread (cost-tolerances-are-derived). Replaces nothing." :fns [] :sections ["docs/cost.md#what-is-measured"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.turn/context-render-observes-what-changed" :merge "11d34aacd" :plan-at "merge" :plan "THE PLAN step 3 (the agent's turn and its context). `seon.turn/observe` called `seon.reconcile/index` on the branch's db every turn: the whole index listed from empty and settled over every task, though `seon.reconcile/done-input` reads only the agent's task's proofs and kind and the claimants of the tests that ran (notes/perf-2026-10-24.md, Next wins 1: 74% of a 115M-tick, 512 MB context render). Its cost also grew as n^1.75 with the number of tasks. Fix at the cause, with the one incremental mechanism that exists, `seon.reconcile/observe`: `seon.reconcile/index` takes the index of an earlier db and observes the later one over the datoms of the watched attributes that differ (`datahike.api/datoms-diff` per attribute range, which skips the stored subtrees both share), and `seon.turn/index!` holds the agent's last index and db in `seon.agent/state` (:index), so only the first turn lists the index. Deletes the per-turn listing; no new mechanism. Measured on a root's main of 680 tasks and 118k datoms: listing 92.0M ticks and 384 MB, observing 1.29M ticks and 10.2 MB, with equal indexes. The cost proof runs on the in-memory test store, where no node has an address and each diff walks its attribute's datoms, so it holds the linear bound and never the stored db's skip of shared subtrees (measured above, once, by hand). Not taken here: the first turn still lists the index (the fork server could hand main's index to the agents it forks, as it hands :graph)." :fns ["seon.reconcile/index" "seon.turn/index!" "seon.turn/observe"] :sections ["docs/turn.md#observe"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.reconcile/an-observed-retraction-leaves-no-residue" "seon.reconcile/octopus-merges-off-as-decided" "seon.turn/a-turn-shows-its-cost-against-its-budget"]}
{:task "datahike.query/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error" :merge "0ddd1141b" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Found 2026-10-23 (branch jolt/nested-call-refused) under task a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused: datahike.query/filter-by-pred wraps each predicate in a local safe-pred fn, which catches ClassCastException and IllegalArgumentException and answers false, so a predicate given a value of the wrong type ([(clojure.string/ends-with? ?n \"x\")] over symbols, a comparison of a string with a number) drops the row without a word, and the query reads as a true empty answer. A swallowed error (AGENTS section 9): what Datomic does (the query throws), whether any Datahike behaviour or test relies on the false (grep its tests for type-mixed predicates), then the fix at the producer: the error raised with the clause and the offending values, or, if a mixed-type column must filter, a typed comparison that never throws. Proof: a datahike.query-test test whose predicate meets a wrong-typed value raises, naming the clause. Fixed 2026-10-23 (worktree session store, Datahike jolt/a-predicate-that-throws-is-an-error 6b668f0, 25cc875): both catch sites, filter-by-pred's safe-pred and the planner's post-filter-preds, now raise :query/call-threw (datahike.query.analyze/call-threw) with the clause, the call's arguments and the cause; a typed error the call threw passes on as is; the fused direct-rel, columnar and secondary-index paths, which caught every Exception as \"not applicable\" and fell back, rethrow it (datahike.query.analyze/call-threw?). Seon's process tests (seon.agent-test, seon.root-test, seon.repl-test, seon.main-test) pass, so no Seon query leaned on the false. Datahike's own query tests were run once on 6b668f0 and failed the same 11 as on d89be47; they were not re-run on 25cc875, because datahike.test.utils needs datahike.index.hitchhiker-tree, which is not on Jolt's source roots. The catch had also covered the built-in comparisons: their CollectionOrder protocol threw on a value of another type than the first. They now order by datahike.datom/compare-value and never throw, as Datomic's comparisons order across types. On Jolt, clojure.string/ends-with? accepts a symbol (it reads its string), so the proof's predicate is pos? on a symbol." :fns [] :sections ["docs/forks.md#datahike"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "jolt.backend/image-registration-is-opt-in" :merge "9bda779cd" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23; Jolt jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in 686aff7d, eba8826a). Jolt registers every fn literal's source for its state image: fnsrc-flush renders the source, reads it back to check it (fnsrc-row-src), emits image-register-fn-form! per literal and wraps the literal in a maker. Seon writes no image. Now an explicit Jolt compile option, not a changed default: *compiler-options* {:image-fn-forms false} makes a top-level form collect no registrations (backend_scheme.clj fnsrc-regs answers nil), its literals emitted as plain closures with their names; absent or true, Jolt is unchanged for every other user. Jolt reads JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS (an EDN map) into *compiler-options*' root at start (post-prelude.ss), as JVM Clojure reads the clojure.compiler.* properties, and Seon sets it in bin/env, so its build, the test runner, root and the agents it forks all compile without registration. Measured: a realistic defn with four literals 5.14 -> 3.47 MB (-33%); a defn of n literals ~1.24 MB and ~172k ticks a literal registering, ~478 KB and ~52k not (-62%, -68%). Known: JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS is no input of a test's reach digest (bin/env fixes it), and Jolt's AOT cache keys a namespace without it, so a unit compiled with registration may be loaded without it and the reverse (harmless to Seon, which writes no image)." :fns [] :sections ["docs/forks.md#jolt" "docs/source.md#shared-build-caches"] :schemas [":seon.config.build/jolt-compiler-options"] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.watch/a-vanished-file-is-a-fact" :merge "78e998765" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Found 2026-10-23: the standing root's watcher died, seon.watch/died: its batch handler threw .../docs/references.md (No such file or directory): root no longer watches the checkout (exit 143). A git merge or bin/update removed the file for a moment while the batch read the wiki: seon.docs/pages listed docs/ and then slurped each file, and the one gone in between threw. Correct, at the producer (root's ruling): one read of a directory's files, seon.lib/dir-texts, which seon.docs/pages and my.root/task-files both call, leaves out a file listed and gone when read, as a deleted file is: the wiki's load retracts that page's sections, a task file left out is not judged (seon.task/load-tx: a file not given is not judged, so its tasks are never cancelled by a moment's absence), and the change that removed it or writes it back makes the next batch. The catch is FileNotFoundException only, named in docs/failure-points.md. Folds the two directory readers the one-offs survey found (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md, item 6)." :fns ["my.root/task-files" "seon.docs/pages" "seon.lib/dir-texts"] :sections ["docs/failure-points.md#edges-that-catch-each-names-its-one-variant"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.cost/ticks-are-exact" :merge "75fa45bf2" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Found 2026-10-23 (worktree session store) under seon.source-test/a-fixed-change-costs-the-same-at-any-program-size: the transact row's ticks differed between runs, 406.8k / 425k / 479.6k at 10 / 100 / 1,000 in one bin/prove shard against 406.3k / 424.8k / 477.1k in another process, 0.5% at 1,000, enough to move a growth fit by 0.02. docs/cost.md calls ticks exact. Reproduction (scratch script, seon.profile/measure! of d/db-with on seon.source-test/transact-of 10, 40 samples per process): within a process the count is exact, 406,355-406,362 per sample. The first call reads 409,285 (warming, which measure's warm call takes). About one sample in 40 reads 1,000 more (Chez's timer quantum, docs/cost.md). Holding 300 MB of live data first changes nothing (no collection ran during any sample). Two causes. (1) Fixed: seon.cost/measure repeated a call until 20 ms of CPU time or 25 calls, a clock deciding how many samples a counted dimension's minimum is taken over: a fast call took 25 and a slow or loaded one 3. It now takes seon.cost/repeats (3) after its warm call, whatever a call costs (seon.cost-test/a-measure-takes-a-count-of-samples-never-a-time: 26 calls before, 4 after). (2) Not yet found: the shard's process counts 0.1-0.5% more ticks for the same call than a fresh one. It is not the heap, and not a collection. It is what ran earlier in that process, e.g. protocol-dispatch caches (Jolt's PIC and resolve-memo, invalidated by every registration), Datahike's global caches, or the store's id counters giving other entity ids and so other index paths. Next: measure the row in one fresh process after requiring and running each other test namespace of its shard, bisect to the one that moves the count, and fix at that state's owner, so the same call counts the same in any process." :fns ["seon.cost/measure" "seon.cost/repeats"] :sections ["docs/cost.md#what-is-measured"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.cost/cost-tolerances-are-derived"]}
{:task "seon.root/the-api-uses-flows-own-channels" :merge "2156f548b" :plan-at "parent" :plan "What exists: (1) `seon.root/start!` starts a thread, seon-flow-errors, that alts!! on one channel (Flow's error chan) and puts each defect on :back as {:op :error :pid :message :data :msg}; `seon.root/stop!` joins it with .join 5000; `seon.check/threads` declares it. (2) The api's step collects outputs in its state (::to-agents, ::replies; root.clj:119, :173) and flushes them through `seon.step/out!` at the step's end (:533-536), beside the step's own outputs volatile. Correct: (1) (a/pipe error-chan (a/chan 1 (map ->op)) ...) into :back: no thread, no join; (2) each site calls `seon.step/out!` on o, as `seon.root/back-op` already does. Deletes the thread, its join and roster entry, the two state keys and the flush. A failure point: \u00a75 allows a thread only for a blocking foreign read." :fns ["seon.root/answer-spawn" "seon.root/answer-taken" "seon.root/api-one" "seon.root/api-proc" "seon.root/back-op" "seon.root/deliver-message" "seon.root/deliver-rid" "seon.root/end-agent" "seon.root/ext-op" "seon.root/record-death" "seon.root/reply!" "seon.root/set-agent" "seon.root/spawn-failed" "seon.root/start!" "seon.root/stop!" "seon.root/to-agent"] :sections ["docs/failure-points.md#entrant-boundaries-a-defect-becomes-a-recorded-error-map" "docs/flow.md#idioms-for-seon"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.test-store/a-kept-store-is-keyed-by-its-schema" :merge "1f19114c1" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Found 2026-10-23 by root: the page merge changed three attributes to refs, and every checkout's kept target/program-store and target/runs/test-store refused to open (seon.db/open!: :seon.db/types-differ, another value type than the schema declares). bin/test failed at its plan, and process tests at root start. Both are regenerable output, made again from their inputs, never records. Fix: seon.db/schema-digest names the schema; seon.test-store/kept-store! answers base-<digest> and removes a store at base of another schema (base itself, or base-<8 hex digits>), sparing a runs directory beside it. The test runner's program store and the process store (seon.test-store/process-store, made by the plan's seon.test-store/make-process-store!, which replaces bin/test's seed step) are named by it. A changed schema makes a new store; nothing is migrated or refused." :fns ["seon.db/schema-digest"] :sections ["docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.test-store/a-test-root-is-its-own"]}
