{:task "seon.profile/a-call-is-measured-one-way" :merge "94eef5f16" :plan-at "merge" :plan "Owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs (DECISIONS). What exists: two measurements of one call. seon.repl/wrap-cost measures each evaluated form (wall ns by seon.profile/now-ns, allocated bytes, engine ticks counted in a finally inside jolt.host/ticks-of so a form that throws is counted, the costliest functions); seon.cost/once measures a cost proof's call (CPU ns, allocated bytes, ticks as ticks-of answers them, contracted calls). Correct: one seon.profile/measure! runs a function and answers its value, and hands what it measured ({:ticks :bytes :wall-ns :cpu-ns :calls}) to a callback from a finally, so a call that throws is measured and its exception goes on; the REPL and the cost proof each read what they show from it. A law keeps it one: no src form but measure! reads the tick counter (jolt.host/ticks-of or ticks). Deletes both inline measurements." :fns ["seon.cost/once" "seon.fx/base-commit" "seon.profile/measure!" "seon.repl/wrap-cost"] :sections ["docs/clocks.md#time-measured-recorded-never-compared-to-decide" "docs/cost.md#where-it-is-used"] :schemas [] :tasks ["seon.profile/one-monotonic-clock"]}
{:task "seon.contract/an-armed-call-costs-its-checks" :merge "7e869f9e3" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Found by worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23. A call of a contracted function costs 354 B and 12 ticks over a plain call (seon.cost/units :fn/armed-call, [:=> [:cat :int] :int]): its argument tuple, the malli validators' work and the profile count. Small helpers called per IR node pay it on every node: seon.program/unit-account calls vsym, invoked (twice) and datum (twice) per call node, 5 armed calls, ~1.8 KB of the 2.8 KB a call node costs (seon.program-cost-test/a-scalar-constant-costs-no-walk); seon.source/import-tx makes 519k contracted calls over src and test (~180 MB, ~6% of its bytes). Question for root before any change: is the cost in the wrapper (malli -instrument's per-call allocation: the args vector, an explain on success?) and so fixed there for every function, or is the rule that a private helper in an inner loop is folded into its caller? Measure first: bytes of one armed call split into args collection, input validation, output validation and the count (seon.contract/wrap). Proofs: a cost proof of an armed call at its floor; seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current with :fn/armed-call re-measured." :fns ["seon.contract/options" "seon.cost/units"] :sections ["docs/forks.md#malli"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.cost/a-speedup-never-reads-as-steeper" :merge "7043130e9" :plan-at "parent" :plan "Found 2026-10-23 (worktree session store, under seon.program-cost-test/a-change-re-folds-only-what-reaches-it): seon.cost/judge fits a dimension's growth on measured less the estimate's :fixed. When a change makes the work cheaper, the fixed part falls under the stated :fixed, and the smallest size's remainder shrinks toward zero, so the fitted exponent jumps. Jolt 0d39edc3 took reach-update from 500k to 427k ticks at 100 and from 1.24M to 1.07M at 1,000, under the estimate at both, and the fit on (427k - 420k, 1.07M - 420k) read n^1.96: red for a speedup. Re-deriving each proof's :fixed after every speedup is churn. Correct, in judge: growth is judged from the difference between sizes, (m(n2) - m(n1)) against the estimate's (e(n2) - e(n1)), with no :fixed subtracted, so a uniform speedup can never read as a steeper growth. Proof: a seon.cost-test case whose rows are another case's minus a constant (a speedup of the fixed part) gives the same steeper verdict, and the reach-update rows above are not steeper." :fns ["seon.cost/growth" "seon.cost/judge"] :sections ["docs/cost.md#how-growth-is-judged"] :schemas [] :tasks []}
{:task "seon.main/docstrings-carry-no-dates-and-no-old-words" :merge "ac65fa497" :plan-at "parent" :plan "What exists: docstrings in `src` carry dated rulings and sessions' provenance, which git keeps (docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept: nothing is a dated log; AGENTS \u00a77: a docstring says what a var is). 24 `src` files hold an ISO date: `seon.cost` (`units`, `tick-allowance`, `ratchet`: measurement dates), `seon.program` (the reach-index docstring near :1085), `seon.agent` (`run-tests-forked`, the test-plan docstring near :456), `seon.reconcile` (`hold-tx`, near :444), `seon.db` (`collect!`: \"owner, 2026-10-22, simplest first\"), `seon.template` (`with-merge`: \"pure-tests 2026-10-23\"), `seon.check` and others. `seon.root/start!`'s docstring calls `:stall-ms` \"a guard\" (AGENTS \u00a79: a guard is the old word, and the stall check is the clock task `seon.root/one-clock`'s). Correct: each docstring states the rule as it holds, the owner's ruling cited as \"DECISIONS, <title>\" where it explains the rule; the measurement kept without its date; `:stall-ms` described as the stall bound it is. Deletes the dated clauses. A law proves it: no docstring of a `src` var holds an ISO date (read from the compiler's facts, `:var/doc`), so a new one is red." :fns ["my.agent/deferred" "my.root/gate!" "my.schema/forms" "my.schema/new-key-file" "seon.agent-proc/stall-ms" "seon.agent/base-graph" "seon.agent/run-selection" "seon.agent/run-tests-forked" "seon.agent/test-plan" "seon.agent/transact!" "seon.config/refuse!" "seon.config/sources" "seon.context/current-state" "seon.cost/exact" "seon.cost/sizes" "seon.cost/tick-allowance" "seon.cost/units" "seon.db/collect!" "seon.db/delete-main!" "seon.db/seed!" "seon.fork-server/main" "seon.fork-server/server-step" "seon.fx/test-command" "seon.merge/merge-tx" "seon.problems/run-rows" "seon.program/banned" "seon.program/divergence" "seon.program/env-readers" "seon.program/load-diff!" "seon.program/process-tests" "seon.program/quoted-